title: 'LWP backend swallows an exception thrown in on_event / on_line: watch() and
  log() return as if the stream ended'
updated: 2026-10-18T06:32:30Z
---

`LWPIO::call_streaming` (lib/Kubernetes/REST/LWPIO.pm, `$self->ua->request($http_req, sub {...})`) hands the data callback to LWP as a content callback. LWP::Protocol::collect catches a `die` from that callback, aborts the transfer and records it only in the `X-Died` / `Client-Aborted` response headers; the response status stays 200. `_response` drops the headers, so `watch()` (lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:3044-3051) passes `_check_response` and returns `$last_rv`, and streaming `log()` (3151-3159) returns normally.

Reproduce without a cluster:

```
perl -Ilib -MKubernetes::REST::LWPIO -MKubernetes::REST::HTTPRequest -e 'my $io=Kubernetes::REST::LWPIO->new; my $r=eval{$io->call_streaming(Kubernetes::REST::HTTPRequest->new(method=>"GET",url=>"file://$ENV{PWD}/cpanfile",headers=>{}), sub { die "boom in on_event\n" })}; print "err=[$@] status=", $r->status, "\n"'
# err=[] status=200
```

Effect: a bug in the caller's `on_event` handler (or a deliberate `die` to leave the watch) vanishes; the documented resume loop reconnects from the returned resourceVersion and silently skips the event that failed. The same headers are set when the connection breaks mid-body, so a truncated one-shot `log()` or `list()` body is also returned as a 200 (the list then fails later in the JSON decoder with an unrelated message). HTTPTinyIO behaves differently (599 with the message as content -> APIError code 599), so the two backends disagree.

Done when: both backends rethrow a callback exception from `call_streaming` (LWP: check `X-Died`), the IO role documents that contract, transport failures (LWP's internal 500 'Can't connect', HTTP::Tiny's 599) are distinguishable from an API server status, and t/13_io_backends.t covers a dying callback for both.

